      <!-- SECTION 9: FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif; color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 30px;">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What are construction chemicals?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Construction chemicals are highly specialized chemical compounds, polymers, and mineral additives integrated into concrete, mortars, and structural interfaces during or after construction to enhance compressive strength, reduce concrete permeability, control moisture ingress, and arrest physical degradation pathways.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. What is the most widely used construction chemical?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Concrete admixtures—specifically Polycarboxylate Ether (PCE) based superplasticizers—are the most widely used construction chemicals. They are valued for their exceptional ability to reduce water demand by over 40% while maintaining fresh concrete workability and flow.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. Why is waterproofing important?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Waterproofing protects concrete from water intrusion, which is the primary driver of concrete carbonation, reinforcing steel corrosion, and structural freeze-thaw cracking. Applying robust cementitious, liquid, or crystalline waterproofing sealants is essential to protecting structural steel reinforcement and prolonging asset life.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. How do Polycarboxylate Ether (PCE) superplasticizers work?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              PCE superplasticizers disperse cement agglomerates through steric hindrance. Their methacrylic acid polymer backbones adsorb onto cement grains, while long, hydrophilic polyethylene glycol (PEG) graft side chains extend into the water phase, physically keeping particles apart without requiring extra water.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. What is crystallization waterproofing?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Crystallization waterproofing involves active silicates reacting with free calcium hydroxide and moisture inside concrete pores. This reaction deposits insoluble Calcium Silicate Hydrate (C-S-H) crystalline needles that physically seal capillary micro-pores, forming a permanent, self-healing barrier against hydrostatic water pressure.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

